## Problem
Server components that fetch data sequentially create waterfall requests, increasing Time to First Byte (TTFB) and overall page load time.

## Key Patterns

### 1. Parallelize Independent Fetches in Server Components

```typescript
// BAD - Sequential waterfall
export default async function Page() {
  const user = await fetchUser();       // ~200ms
  const products = await fetchProducts(); // ~300ms
  const reviews = await fetchReviews();   // ~200ms
  // Total: ~700ms

  return <Component user={user} products={products} reviews={reviews} />;
}

// GOOD - Parallel fetches
export default async function Page() {
  const [user, products, reviews] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUser(),       // ~200ms
    fetchProducts(),   // ~300ms (runs concurrently)
    fetchReviews(),    // ~200ms (runs concurrently)
  ]);
  // Total: ~300ms (max of all three)

  return <Component user={user} products={products} reviews={reviews} />;
}
```

### 2. Pre-compute Data on the Server
Move expensive data transformations from client to server components:

```typescript
// BAD - Send raw data to client, compute there
export default async function Page() {
  const reviews = await fetchReviews(); // large array
  return <ProductList reviews={reviews} />; // sends all reviews to browser
}

// GOOD - Pre-compute on server, send minimal data
export default async function Page() {
  const reviews = await fetchReviews();
  const reviewCounts: Record<string, number> = {};
  for (const r of reviews) {
    reviewCounts[r.productId] = (reviewCounts[r.productId] || 0) + 1;
  }
  return <ProductList reviewCounts={reviewCounts} />; // sends small map
}
```

### 3. Remove `force-dynamic` for Cacheable Data
```typescript
// If data updates infrequently, use ISR
export const revalidate = 60;

// Or remove the dynamic export entirely for static generation
```

## Checklist
- [ ] Are independent server-side fetches parallelized with Promise.all?
- [ ] Is data pre-computed on the server before passing to client components?
- [ ] Is the minimal required data sent to client components?
- [ ] Is caching strategy appropriate (static, ISR, or dynamic)?

# Next.js Dynamic Imports for Code Splitting

## Problem
Components that use heavy libraries (lodash, mathjs, chart libraries) increase the initial JavaScript bundle, slowing down page load even if the user never views that component.

## Key Patterns

### 1. Dynamic Import with `next/dynamic`
Split heavy components into separate chunks loaded on demand:

```typescript
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

// Component is loaded only when rendered
const HeavyChart = dynamic(() => import('./HeavyChart'), {
  loading: () => <div>Loading chart...</div>,
});

export default function Page() {
  const [showChart, setShowChart] = useState(false);
  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setShowChart(true)}>Show Chart</button>
      {showChart && <HeavyChart />}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 2. Lazy-Load Tab Content
When using tabs, only load the active tab's component:

```typescript
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

const AdvancedAnalysis = dynamic(() => import('./AdvancedAnalysis'), {
  loading: () => <div className="p-6 text-center">Loading analysis...</div>,
});

function Page() {
  const [tab, setTab] = useState('overview');

  return (
    <div>
      {tab === 'overview' ? <Overview /> : <AdvancedAnalysis />}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 3. Named Exports with Dynamic Imports
For modules with named exports:

```typescript
const Component = dynamic(() =>
  import('./module').then(mod => mod.NamedExport)
);
```

## When to Use
- Components behind user interactions (tabs, modals, accordions)
- Components using heavy third-party libraries
- Below-the-fold content
- Admin/settings panels rarely accessed

## Checklist
- [ ] Are heavy library components dynamically imported?
- [ ] Do dynamic imports have appropriate loading states?
- [ ] Is tab content lazy-loaded?

